# Logging and recovery — Carol's summary

- Log first: the log record describing a change reaches disk before the changed page does.
- Commit = the commit record is durable. Pages can be written later (no-force), and dirty pages of running transactions may be written early (steal).
- After a crash (ARIES): analysis from the last checkpoint → redo everything since the oldest dirty page ("repeat history") → undo transactions that never committed, logging CLRs.
- pageLSN on every page makes redo safe to repeat.
- Postgres: WAL + full-page writes; standbys replay the same stream.
